---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import GrowthCalculator from '../../components/GrowthCalculator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Database Table Growth Calculator" description="Project row count and storage size at 3, 6, 12, and 24 months based on current growth rate.">
  <GrowthCalculator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why projecting growth prevents surprises</h2>
  <p>A table growing steadily today can quietly cross thresholds that matter — index performance degradation, storage cost jumps, or backup window overruns — well before anyone notices, unless someone projects forward from the current growth rate.</p>

  <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your current row count and daily growth rate.</li>
    <li>Enter your average row size in bytes.</li>
    <li>See projected row count and storage size at future milestones.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Example</h2>
  <p>A table with 100,000 rows growing by 500 rows/day at 300 bytes/row will hold roughly 145,000 rows and about 0.04GB in 3 months — useful for spotting when storage or performance planning needs attention well ahead of time.</p>

  <h2>Common Table Growth Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming linear growth continues indefinitely</strong> — real growth often accelerates or plateaus as a product's user base changes, so treat projections as directional, not guaranteed.</li>
    <li><strong>Forgetting index size grows alongside table size</strong>, sometimes disproportionately, and isn't captured by a raw row-size calculation alone.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Growth Calculator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Planning when a table might need partitioning or archiving strategy</li>
    <li>Forecasting storage costs for capacity planning</li>
    <li>Justifying a database scaling investment with concrete projected numbers</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Database Growth Projection FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Does this account for indexes and overhead?</strong> No — this estimates raw row data only; actual database storage will be higher once indexes and internal overhead are included.</p>
</ToolLayout>
